---
source: tests/cli.rs
expression: help
---
Convert raster images into maximally compact SVG, emitting native shape primitives wherever a traced
region is one.

Usage: vectorise [OPTIONS] <INPUT>...

Arguments:
  <INPUT>...
          Raster files.
          
          Globs are normally expanded by your shell; a literal pattern (e.g. quoted `'**/*.png'`) is
          expanded by vectorise itself.

Options:
  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

  -V, --version
          Print version

Output:
  -o, --output-dir <DIR>
          Write all outputs into DIR (flattened). Default: next to each input

  -f, --force
          Overwrite existing outputs. Duplicate outputs still fail

  -n, --dry-run
          Print the input to output plan and exit

      --keep-size
          Emit width and height on the root element as well as viewBox

Tracing:
      --background <COLOR>
          Color that transparency is resolved against, as `#rrggbb` or `#rgb`.
          
          Fully transparent pixels are dropped by the tracer, so this mainly decides what
          semi-transparent pixels become.
          
          [default: #ffffff]

      --preset <PRESET>
          Starting point tuned for a kind of input

          Possible values:
          - bw:     Black-and-white line art
          - poster: Flat, poster-like color with a full palette
          - photo:  Photographic input: heavier speckle filtering, coarser layering
          - auto:   Choose for the user. v1 picks `Poster`; see ADR-0003
          
          [default: auto]

      --clustering <CLUSTERING>
          Region-forming algorithm

          Possible values:
          - color-cluster: Hierarchical color clustering. VTracer's classic path
          - bw:            Threshold to black and white, then cluster the foreground
          - watershed:     Hierarchical watershed on the pixel graph

      --hierarchical <HIERARCHICAL>
          Stacked layers or a seam-free mosaic

          Possible values:
          - stacked: Trace each layer and stack them in paint order
          - cutout:  Seam-free mosaic: each shared boundary is fitted once

  -m, --mode <MODE>
          Curve-fitting mode

          Possible values:
          - pixel:   Exact pixel-lattice polyline, no smoothing
          - polygon: Douglas-Peucker polygon: straight edges, fewer points
          - spline:  Corner detection plus least-squares cubics

      --filter-speckle <PX>
          Discard speckles smaller than this side length, in pixels

      --color-precision <BITS>
          Significant bits per RGB channel

      --gradient-step <N>
          Color difference between gradient layers

      --max-colors <N>
          Quantize to at most this many colors

      --palette <COLORS>
          Fixed palette, as comma-separated hex colors

      --palette-file <FILE>
          Fixed palette, read from a file of hex colors

      --simplify <PX>
          Curve simplification tolerance in pixels. Try 1 to 2.5

      --threshold <N>
          Black-and-white cutoff for `--clustering bw`

      --adaptive
          Use adaptive thresholding instead of a fixed cutoff

      --watershed-detail <N>
          Where to cut the watershed hierarchy. Higher keeps more regions

      --corner-threshold <DEG>
          Corner threshold in degrees. Higher smooths through sharper turns

      --segment-length <PX>
          Segment length threshold in pixels.
          
          Lower it to fit large smooth curves more closely; the default leaves a 400 by 100 oval
          several percent too fat to be recognized as an ellipse.

Shapes:
      --shapes <SHAPES>
          Detect native SVG shapes, or leave every region a path

          Possible values:
          - auto: Detect circles, ellipses, and rectangles
          - off:  Emit every region as a path
          
          [default: auto]

      --shape-tolerance <FRACTION>
          Allowed difference between a shape and the outline it replaces, as a fraction of the
          region's area
          
          [default: 0.02]

      --min-shape-area <PX2>
          Regions smaller than this many square pixels stay paths
          
          [default: 16]

      --no-rotated-ellipses
          Keep rotated ellipses as paths instead of emitting a rotation

Output quality:
      --no-optimize
          Skip the optimizer pass.
          
          The writer already emits minimal markup; the optimizer rewrites path data on top of that.
          Turn it off to keep the output exactly as the writer produced it.

      --verify
          Render the result and report how close it came to the input

      --stats
          Report what the conversion did, one line per file plus a total

      --stats-json
          Report what the conversion did as one JSON object per line, on stdout

  -p, --precision <N>
          Decimal places kept on every coordinate
          
          [default: 2]

Runtime:
  -j, --jobs <N>
          Number of files converted at once. Default: available parallelism

  -q, --quiet
          Report nothing but errors

  -v, --verbose...
          Report more: once for info, twice for debug, three times for trace
